<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Max von Oppenheim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Max von Oppenheim was a German diplomat, orientalist and archaeologist. In 1899 he discovered the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-halaf">Tell Halaf</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, ancient Guzana, which he excavated from 1911 to 1913 and in 1929. He displayed the finds in his own museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where they were shattered by bombing in 1943; the fragments were reassembled and exhibited again in 2011.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-von-oppenheim.jpg" alt="Max von Oppenheim" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oppenheim_Circa1917.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Oppenheim was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, the son of Albert Oppenheim, of the Jewish family of bankers, and Pauline Engels, of a Cologne merchant family. His grandfather Simon was made a baron in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1867, and the family took the name von Oppenheim. Max studied law at Strasbourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, passed his examinations in Cologne in 1883 and learned Arabic while training as a lawyer. In 1892 he spent seven months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and in 1893–94 he crossed the Syrian desert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, the journey of his travelogue Vom Mittelmeer zum Persischen Golf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rejected at first by the Foreign Office because of his father's Jewish background, he became an attaché at the German consulate-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1896; the British press called him a master spy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>. Surveying a route for the Baghdad Railway for Deutsche Bank, he found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-halaf">Tell Halaf</a> on 19 November 1899 from villagers' tales of stone idols under the sand, and reburied the statues, as he had no permit to dig.</p><p>His campaign of 1911, with five archaeologists, more than 500 local workers and a small steam train, cost about 750,000 marks, paid by his father. Besides the basalt statues and reliefs of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapara">Kapara</a>'s Western Palace, it found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> painted pottery of about 6,000 to 5,000 BCE, which gave its name to the Halaf culture. In October 1914 he wrote a memorandum urging that the Ottoman sultan be enlisted to call the world's Muslims to holy war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, and he headed the Intelligence Bureau for the East in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He lost most of his wealth in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> of 1923. In 1927 he won a division of his finds with the authorities of the French Mandate: his share went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and the rest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, where the museum he installed became the nucleus of the National Museum. Under Nazi rule, according to the historian Sean McMeekin, he ascribed his statues to Aryan culture. Bombing in 1943 destroyed his apartment and much of his library; he lived through the firebombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> in February 1945 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, where his grave is a basalt replica of the seated woman statue he admired.</p><p>The broken basalt pieces lay untouched in the cellars of the Pergamon Museum under the GDR. After reunification the Vorderasiatisches Museum rebuilt from 2001 to 2010 more than 30 sculptures from around 27,000 fragments, shown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 2011 and at the Bundeskunsthalle Bonn in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Max von Oppenheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Excavated by <b>Max von Oppenheim</b> from 1911, they were shattered by bombing in Berlin in 1943 and painstakingly restored.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-bahiani">Bit Bahiani</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.94,6.96&amp;year=1915" aria-label="Cologne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Max von Oppenheim"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5374 5752h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5374 5752h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.94,6.96&amp;year=1915">What was here in 1915? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
